<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <%- include('partials/head') %>
</head>
<body>
  <%- include('partials/cursor') %>
  <%- include('partials/nav') %>

  <!-- Hero -->
  <section class="pitch-hero">
    <div class="pitch-hero-inner reveal">
      <span class="pitch-hero-badge">Short Film &middot; Drama</span>
      <h1 class="pitch-hero-title">Wings of the<br>Fallen</h1>
      <p class="pitch-hero-byline">Written by Alasdair Braxton</p>
      <p class="pitch-hero-logline">After losing his closest friend to a reckless leap of freedom, a young soldier learns to carry grief with quiet dignity &mdash; and years later, teaches the son of the officer who once denied him leave to say goodbye how to fly in his friend's place.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Facts -->
  <div class="pitch-facts">
    <div class="pitch-fact">
      <span class="pitch-fact-label">Format</span>
      <span class="pitch-fact-value">Short Film</span>
    </div>
    <div class="pitch-fact">
      <span class="pitch-fact-label">Genre</span>
      <span class="pitch-fact-value">British Military Drama</span>
    </div>
    <div class="pitch-fact">
      <span class="pitch-fact-label">Setting</span>
      <span class="pitch-fact-value">Isle of Arran, Scotland</span>
    </div>
    <div class="pitch-fact">
      <span class="pitch-fact-label">Status</span>
      <span class="pitch-fact-value">In Development</span>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Synopsis -->
  <section class="pitch-section">
    <div class="pitch-section-inner reveal">
      <span class="pitch-eyebrow">The Story</span>
      <h2 class="pitch-heading">Some losses you never forget. Some wings you learn to carry forward.</h2>
      <div class="pitch-body">
        <p>Fred Jones and Liam Brown are brothers in everything but blood &mdash; two soldiers who've shared the same mud and the same fear, and who spend their leave chasing the wind off the cliffs of Arran. When Fred is called back to base early, Liam stays behind for one more jump. He doesn't come back.</p>
        <p>What follows isn't a story about combat. It's about the harder fight that comes after: a young soldier learning to carry grief inside a system built on restraint, denied even the right to grieve at Liam's funeral. Years later, Fred &mdash; now a mentor to a new generation of recruits &mdash; meets a private who reminds him, unmistakably, of the friend he lost. What he chooses to pass on becomes the film's quiet, aching answer to everything he couldn't say the first time.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="pitch-comps">
        <span class="pitch-comp-chip">In the vein of Kajaki: The True Story</span>
        <span class="pitch-comp-chip">In the vein of Ordinary Love</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Characters -->
  <section class="pitch-characters">
    <div class="pitch-characters-inner reveal">
      <span class="pitch-eyebrow">Who's In It</span>
      <h2 class="pitch-heading">Character Breakdown</h2>
      <div class="pitch-characters-grid">
        <div class="pitch-character">
          <span class="pitch-character-name">Fred Jones</span>
          <p class="pitch-character-desc">Solid, grounded, disciplined to a fault. Carries grief the only way he knows how &mdash; by carrying on. Our way into a story about strength that doesn't look like strength.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="pitch-character">
          <span class="pitch-character-name">Liam Brown</span>
          <p class="pitch-character-desc">Restless, alive, allergic to sitting still. The friend whose absence shapes every scene after he's gone &mdash; present in a pair of gloves, a compass, a half-remembered tune.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="pitch-character">
          <span class="pitch-character-name">Oliver Cooper</span>
          <p class="pitch-character-desc">A young recruit with Liam's same wit, drive, and terrible singing voice, and no idea what he's stepped into. Fred's chance to give someone what he never got to give Liam.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="pitch-character">
          <span class="pitch-character-name">Sergeant Cooper</span>
          <p class="pitch-character-desc">The officer who denied Fred leave to grieve, and years later watches his own son learn to fly under the man he once turned away.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Director's Statement -->
  <section class="pitch-quote">
    <div class="pitch-quote-inner reveal">
      <span class="pitch-quote-mark">&ldquo;</span>
      <p class="pitch-quote-text">Wings of the Fallen is written for anyone who's loved and lost within a system that teaches restraint. I want audiences to leave the film reflective but hopeful &mdash; recognising that grief is universal, and that redemption often lies in compassion, not control. Strength can look like vulnerability, and honour extends beyond the battlefield.</p>
      <cite class="pitch-quote-cite">Alasdair Braxton, Writer</cite>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- CTA -->
  <section class="pitch-cta">
    <div class="pitch-cta-inner reveal">
      <h2 class="pitch-cta-heading">Read the Full Script</h2>
      <p class="pitch-cta-text">Wings of the Fallen is currently in development and seeking production partners. Get in touch to request the full script.</p>
      <a href="mailto:<%= contactEmail %>?subject=Wings%20of%20the%20Fallen%20%E2%80%94%20Script%20Request" class="pitch-cta-btn">Request the Script</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <%- include('partials/footer') %>
  <%- include('partials/scripts') %>
</body>
</html>
